        "content": "<p>Hello ArkLib community,</p>\n<p>First, for brevity, “interleaved codes” here means interleaved codes of linear codes, as commonly used in SNARK papers.</p>\n<p>We currently have the definition of linear code as follow:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>CodingTheorey/Basic.lean</code>:  <code>abbrev LinearCode.{u, v} (ι : Type u) [Fintype ι] (F : Type v) [Semiring F] : Type (max u v) := Submodule F (ι → F)</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>I've been formalizing some results that rely on interleaved codes and noticed that our current definitions (like <code>MatrixSubmodule</code>, <code>LawfulInterleavedCode</code>) are structured \"row-wise\" (<code>Submodule F (Matrix κ ι F)</code>, i.e., <code>Submodule F (κ → ι → F)</code>).</p>\n<p>This seems to be the transpose of how interleaved codes are typically defined and used in the literature, where the <em>columns</em> are the symbols of the new code. For example:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition 4.3 (Interleaved code), Ligero</strong>. Let <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L \\subset \\mathbb{F}^n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7224em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⊂</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">F</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> be an <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[n, k, d]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03148em;\">k</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span> linear code over <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{F}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">F</span></span></span></span>. We let <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L^m</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">m</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> denote the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[n, m k, d]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03148em;\">mk</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span> (interleaved) code over <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{F}^m</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">F</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">m</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> whose codewords are all <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m \\times n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">m</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span> matrices <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10903em;\">U</span></span></span></span> such that every row <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_i</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10903em;\">U</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.109em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10903em;\">U</span></span></span></span> satisfies <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U_i \\in L</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10903em;\">U</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.109em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span></span></span></span>. For <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U \\in L^m</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7224em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10903em;\">U</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">L</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">m</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j \\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.854em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.05724em;\">j</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span>, we denote by <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U[j]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10903em;\">U</span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.05724em;\">j</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span> the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.854em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.05724em;\">j</span></span></span></span> th symbol (column) of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.10903em;\">U</span></span></span></span>.<ul>\n<li><strong>WHIR (Definition 3.3)</strong> and <strong>Ligero-Binius (Section 2.2)</strong> use similar definitions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The current definitions of us use structure &amp; subtype wrappers, which are not first-class Set/Submodule definition. They don't fit into the current definition of <code>Code</code>: <code>abbrev Code.{u, v} (ι : Type u) (S : Type v) : Type (max u v) := Set (ι → S)</code>, so it's not clean to inherit the lemmas for generic code.</p>\n<p>Interleaved code is an important concept that exists in a lot of in coding theory for SNARKs, e.g. correlated agreement/proximity gaps, tensor-style proximity test protocols, etc. I'd like to propose a refactor for better compatibility of it based on two key ideas:</p>\n<h3>1. Model <code>InterleavedCode</code> column-wise</h3>\n<p>Instead of modeling the code as a submodule of row-indexed functions <code>Submodule F (κ → ι → F)</code>, we should model it as a submodule of column-indexed functions: <code>Submodule F (ι → (κ → F))</code></p>\n<p>This means a codeword is a function from a column index <code>ι</code> to a \"symbol,\" where the symbol is the column itself (<code>κ → A</code>).</p>\n<p><strong>Why this definition:</strong></p>\n<p>* <strong>Natural Alignment:</strong> This directly matches the <code>Code</code> definition (<code>Set (ι → F)</code>) and is similar to <code>LinearCode</code> (<code>Submodule F (ι → F)</code>), where <code>ι</code> is the index type (length) and <code>F</code> is the symbol type (alphabet).<br>\n  * <strong>API Reuse:</strong> We can instantly apply the entire API for <code>Code</code>, <code>Code.dist</code>, <code>Code.minDist</code>, etc., to <code>InterleavedCode</code> with <em>no wrappers or translation layers</em>. The current row-wise definition treats the matrix as a stack of words and redefine properties like distance column-by-column.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">BEFORE</span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e₀₀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e₀₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e₀₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e₁₀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e₁₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e₁₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">κ</span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"n\">indexed</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e₂₀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e₂₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e₂₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"n\">AFTER</span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"n\">indexed</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">↓</span><span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"bp\">↓</span><span class=\"w\">     </span><span class=\"bp\">↓</span>\n<span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e₀₀</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"n\">e₀₁</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"n\">e₀₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e₁₀</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"n\">e₁₁</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"n\">e₁₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">e₂₀</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"n\">e₂₁</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"n\">e₂₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<h3>2. Introduce a <code>ModuleCode</code> abstraction</h3>\n<p>To make this more seamless, I suggest a generalization of <code>LinearCode</code> to <code>ModuleCode</code>, where the alphabet <code>A</code> can be different from scalar set <code>F</code> and <code>A</code> is a module over <code>F</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">universe</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">κ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Fintype</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Semiring</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">AddCommMonoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/--</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">A `ModuleCode ι F A` is an `F`-linear code of length indexed by `ι`</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">over the alphabet `A`, defined as an `F`-submodule of `ι → A`.</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">-/</span>\n<span class=\"n\">abbrev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ModuleCode</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Semiring</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">AddCommMonoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">max</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">letI</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Module</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">apply</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">Pi</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">module</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">I</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">Submodule</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- We can easily recover LinearCode</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">LinearCode_is_ModuleCode</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">LinearCode</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ModuleCode</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Why this definition:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Backward-compatibility with broader definition:</strong> <code>ModuleCode</code>inherits everything from <code>Code</code>. And <code>LinearCode</code> is just <code>ModuleCode ι F F</code>. We are likely to be able to lift some generic definitions for <code>LinearCode</code> to <code>ModuleCode</code>, or we don't have to do anything with <code>LinearCode</code> at all, nothing affected.</li>\n<li><strong>More generic interleaved code</strong>: First, interleaved codes of linear codes is naturally a module code, but not a linear code. Second, with <code>ModuleCode</code>, we can generalize the interleaved codes further to <strong>interleaved codes of module codes</strong>, instead of interleaved codes of linear codes, both ways result in module codes by definition (but not linear code). This way, we can write generic definitions such as proximity gaps for module code and use it for interleaved codes (e.g. <a href=\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1351\">DG25</a>, etc), in which the randomness is sampled from the scalar set, not the symbol set.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">MC</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ModuleCode</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- Interleaved code submodule of any ModuleCode -/</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ModuleCode.interleavedCodeSubmodule</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Submodule</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Matrix</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">κ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">carrier</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">InterleavedWord</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">κ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V.getRow</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MC</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">add_mem'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hU</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hV</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MC.add_mem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hU</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hV</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">zero_mem'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MC.zero_mem</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">smul_mem'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hV</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MC.smul_mem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hV</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- Interleaved code of any ModuleCode, where κ is the row index set: `Submodule F (ι → (κ → A))`</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">-/</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ModuleCode.InterleavedCode</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">κ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">MC</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ModuleCode</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ModuleCode</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">κ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ModuleCode.interleavedCodeSubmodule</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MC</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">omit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Fintype</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Nonempty</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Fintype</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Fintype</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">DecidableEq</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mem_MCInterleavedCode</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">U</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">InterleavedWord</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">κ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">MC</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ModuleCode</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">F</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">U</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MC.InterleavedCode</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">κ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">U.getRow</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MC</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li><strong>Interoperability:</strong> Definitions and lemmas for <code>Code</code> and <code>ModuleCode</code>can be used for <code>LinearCode</code> and <code>InterleavedCode</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This refactor seems to offer a more natural alignment with the literature, increase compatibility, and makes our coding theory library more general and composable.</p>\n<p>What do people think of this approach? I’m happy to move forward with a PR if the consensus is that this direction makes sense.</p>",
        "id": 554053955,
        "sender_full_name": "Chung Thai Nguyen",
        "timestamp": 1762425279
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks for this thoughtful &amp; detailed proposal. I am 100% on board.</p>\n<p>If there's no objection in the next few days, you should go ahead and do it.</p>",
        "id": 554130246,
        "sender_full_name": "Quang Dao",
        "timestamp": 1762445073
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Hey <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"815212\">@Chung Thai Nguyen</span>, thanks so much for the long message! I'll look into it in proper detail an hour or so; I just wanted to make a preemptive comment that the codewords of the interleaved code are not exactly the columns. If you start with a linear code of length <code>\\iota</code> and want to form an interleaved code of length <code>\\kappa</code>, the codewords would be the <code>\\kappa \\x \\iota</code> matrices. So in a sense, you can think of this as picking <code>\\kappa</code> codewords and putting them as rows of a matrix. Then this is viewed as a code of length <code>\\kappa</code> (which is why we had the row formulation), if this makes sense? </p>\n<p>However, the caveat with why the columns are important and might seem more convenient to do your way is that you reason about the distance of the interleaved code columnwise, intuitively, to detect the max possible distance of the list of codewords you used to build your interleaved codeword (which is hy the columns appear in the def). But mathematically, they are not the codewords if this makes sense).</p>",
        "id": 554145128,
        "sender_full_name": "Katy Hristova",
        "timestamp": 1762448770
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks for the derailed overview! This seems spot on so I'm also on board.</p>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"879719\">@Katy Hristova</span> </p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Then this is viewed as a code of length <code>\\kappa</code> (which is why we had the row formulation), if this makes sense?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I think this isn't quite right: I believe what changes is the alphabet, not the length (which is the same as that of the base code).<br>\nThe current row-first formulation seems fine/better for constructing the interleaved code, but the column-first formulation would be nicer for computing distances, which I think we care about more when it comes to proving theorems.</p>",
        "id": 554152461,
        "sender_full_name": "Alexander Hicks",
        "timestamp": 1762450864
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Sure. <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"690355\">@Alexander Hicks</span> mentioned important points about the alphabet. Here I add some clarifications on my proposal when <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"879719\">@Katy Hristova</span>  look at it.</p>\n<p>Intuitively, each interleaved word is a matrix of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">κ</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">κ</span></span></span></span> x <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ι</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">ι</span></span></span></span> cells. Each interleaved codeword is similarly a matrix of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">κ</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">κ</span></span></span></span> x <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ι</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">ι</span></span></span></span> cells with an additional contraint that each row of it is a codeword in the base linear code. This additional constraint is baked into the definition of Submodule of the interleaved code (I've given in the proposal).</p>\n<p>Even though I propose to represent an interleaved word/codeword column-wise (i.e. <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ι</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">ι</span></span></span></span> x <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">κ</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">κ</span></span></span></span>), we still have the ROW-WISE constraints that:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>There are <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">κ</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">κ</span></span></span></span> rows (from the human-view). For interleaved codewords, each row is a codeword.</li>\n<li>Each column of it is a symbol of the interleaved code.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So no conflict in definition here, whether it's in your case or in my case, just a different representation for convenience &amp; compatibility. We don't change the mathematical definition/meaning of the interleaved code. We simply view interleaved codewords using the appropriate alphabet, as Alex mentioned.</p>\n<p>In terms of interleaved codeword, it's usually easier to visualize it as a matrix where each row is a codeword. We can achieve that view via simple abstraction (e.g. functions or getRow(), getCol(), etc). Anyway, based on what I've seen so far, related papers usually view an interleaved codeword as a vector of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ι</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">ι</span></span></span></span> symbols by definition. I hope this make it clear.</p>",
        "id": 554155838,
        "sender_full_name": "Chung Thai Nguyen",
        "timestamp": 1762451875
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"690355\">@Alexander Hicks</span> oh yes, this is right re: the length of the code, my mistake with confusing <code>n</code> and <code>m</code> above</p>",
        "id": 554156550,
        "sender_full_name": "Katy Hristova",
        "timestamp": 1762452082
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"815212\">@Chung Thai Nguyen</span> oh, did you initially mean each column is a symbol, i.e. a letter of the new interleaved alphabet? In which case I agree!</p>",
        "id": 554157605,
        "sender_full_name": "Katy Hristova",
